-- CySA+ (CS0-003) flashcards. Domains: 1 Security Operations (33%),
-- 2 Vulnerability Management (30%), 3 Incident Response & Management (20%),
-- 4 Reporting & Communication (17%).
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- Domain 1: Security Operations
('cy-f-001','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the difference between a threat, a vulnerability, and a risk?$$,$$Threat: an actor/event that can cause harm. Vulnerability: a weakness that can be exploited. Risk: the likelihood and impact of a threat exploiting a vulnerability.$$),
('cy-f-002','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What does EDR provide that traditional antivirus does not?$$,$$Continuous endpoint telemetry, behavioral detection, investigation timelines, and response actions (isolate host, kill process, roll back) - not just signature blocking.$$),
('cy-f-003','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is UEBA and what does it detect?$$,$$User and Entity Behavior Analytics baselines normal behavior and flags anomalies (odd hours, mass downloads, impossible travel) - catches insider threats and compromised accounts.$$),
('cy-f-004','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the purpose of a SIEM in security operations?$$,$$Aggregate logs from many sources, normalize them, correlate events across sources, and alert - plus retention for investigations and compliance dashboards.$$),
('cy-f-005','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the difference between IOCs and IOAs?$$,$$Indicators of Compromise are artifacts of a breach that already happened (hashes, IPs, domains). Indicators of Attack focus on attacker behavior/intent in progress (e.g., lateral movement patterns).$$),
('cy-f-006','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is beaconing and how is it detected?$$,$$Regular callbacks from a compromised host to a C2 server. Detected via network flow analysis: periodic, similar-size connections to the same external host at fixed intervals.$$),
('cy-f-007','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the MITRE ATT&CK framework?$$,$$A knowledge base of adversary tactics (the why/goal) and techniques (the how) observed in real attacks, used to map detections and assess coverage.$$),
('cy-f-008','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the Cyber Kill Chain?$$,$$Lockheed Martin's 7 stages: Reconnaissance, Weaponization, Delivery, Exploitation, Installation, Command & Control, Actions on Objectives. Break any link to stop the attack.$$),
('cy-f-009','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis?$$,$$Analyzes intrusions across four vertices: Adversary, Capability, Infrastructure, and Victim - linking events to understand campaigns.$$),
('cy-f-010','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is TTP in threat intelligence?$$,$$Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures - the behavioral patterns of a threat actor. Harder for attackers to change than IOCs, so more durable for detection.$$),
('cy-f-011','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the pyramid of pain?$$,$$Ranks indicators by how much pain denying them causes an attacker: hash values (trivial) up through IPs, domains, tools, to TTPs (hardest to change).$$),
('cy-f-012','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is proactive threat hunting?$$,$$Hypothesis-driven searching through telemetry for undetected compromises, rather than waiting for alerts. Assumes breach and looks for evidence.$$),
('cy-f-013','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is the value of threat intelligence feeds?$$,$$They supply current IOCs, TTPs, and actor context (STIX/TAXII formats) so detections stay aligned with active campaigns and known-bad indicators.$$),
('cy-f-014','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What are STIX and TAXII?$$,$$STIX: a standardized language for describing threat intelligence. TAXII: the transport protocol for sharing STIX data between systems.$$),
('cy-f-015','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is packet capture (PCAP) analysis used for?$$,$$Full-content inspection of network traffic - reconstructing sessions, extracting files, confirming exfiltration - when flow metadata is insufficient.$$),
('cy-f-016','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What does a WAF protect and at which layer?$$,$$Web applications at Layer 7 (HTTP/S) - filtering SQLi, XSS, and other app-layer attacks that network firewalls cannot see.$$),
('cy-f-017','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is DNS sinkholing?$$,$$Redirecting malicious domain lookups to a controlled IP, cutting off C2 callbacks and revealing which internal hosts are infected.$$),
('cy-f-018','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the difference between north-south and east-west traffic?$$,$$North-south: traffic entering/leaving the network perimeter. East-west: lateral traffic between internal hosts - key for detecting lateral movement.$$),
('cy-f-019','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is living off the land (LOTL)?$$,$$Attackers using legitimate built-in tools (PowerShell, WMI, PsExec) to blend in and evade detection, leaving little malware on disk.$$),
('cy-f-020','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is sandbox analysis of malware?$$,$$Detonating a suspicious file in an isolated environment to observe behavior (files, registry, network) without risking production.$$),
('cy-f-021','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the purpose of a honeypot in security operations?$$,$$A decoy system that attracts attackers so their techniques can be observed and any interaction flags as malicious (no legitimate reason to touch it).$$),
('cy-f-022','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the difference between structured and unstructured threat data?$$,$$Structured: machine-readable, standardized (STIX, CSV feeds). Unstructured: free-text (reports, blogs) requiring human/NLP interpretation.$$),
('cy-f-023','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is endpoint isolation/quarantine and when is it used?$$,$$Cutting a compromised host off the network (usually via EDR) to contain a threat while preserving it for investigation.$$),
('cy-f-024','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What are the main email header fields useful in phishing analysis?$$,$$Return-Path, Received (hop chain), SPF/DKIM/DMARC results, Reply-To vs From mismatches, and originating IP - to trace spoofing.$$),
('cy-f-025','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a false positive vs false negative in detection?$$,$$False positive: a benign event flagged as malicious (alert fatigue). False negative: a real attack not detected (silent risk).$$),
('cy-f-026','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is the intelligence cycle?$$,$$Planning/Direction, Collection, Processing, Analysis, Dissemination, Feedback - the loop that turns raw data into actionable intelligence.$$),
('cy-f-027','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is deception technology beyond honeypots?$$,$$Honeytokens, honeyfiles, decoy credentials, and fake network services planted to detect and misdirect attackers who touch them.$$),
('cy-f-028','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the difference between a tactic and a technique in ATT&CK?$$,$$A tactic is the adversary's goal (e.g., Persistence). A technique is how they achieve it (e.g., scheduled task). Sub-techniques refine further.$$),
('cy-f-029','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is SOAR and how does it relate to a SIEM?$$,$$SOAR orchestrates tools and runs automated response playbooks; the SIEM detects and alerts. SOAR acts on what the SIEM finds.$$),
('cy-f-030','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a heat map / risk matrix in security monitoring?$$,$$A visual plotting likelihood against impact to prioritize alerts, vulnerabilities, or risks by severity.$$),
('cy-f-031','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is NetFlow and what does it capture?$$,$$Metadata about network conversations (source, destination, ports, bytes, timing) without payloads - scalable for spotting anomalies and beaconing.$$),
('cy-f-032','cysa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the analysis benefit of log correlation across sources?$$,$$Linking related events (failed logins + new admin account + data transfer) into one high-fidelity detection that single logs would miss.$$),
('cy-f-033','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is OSINT in threat intelligence?$$,$$Open-Source Intelligence - gathering from public sources (WHOIS, social media, breach dumps, vendor advisories) to profile threats or targets.$$),
-- Domain 2: Vulnerability Management
('cy-f-034','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What are the phases of the vulnerability management lifecycle?$$,$$Identification (scan/asset discovery), Analysis (validate, prioritize), Response/Remediation, and Validation (rescan), feeding continuous reporting.$$),
('cy-f-035','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between active and passive scanning?$$,$$Active: probes targets directly (accurate, can disrupt fragile systems). Passive: observes network traffic (safe, no disruption, less complete).$$),
('cy-f-036','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$Credentialed vs non-credentialed scan - which is more accurate and why?$$,$$Credentialed scans log in and inspect installed software/configs/patches from inside - far fewer false results than the outside-in non-credentialed view.$$),
('cy-f-037','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What does CVSS base score measure, and what are its metric groups?$$,$$Intrinsic severity 0-10. Groups: Base (constant characteristics), Temporal (changes over time, e.g., exploit maturity), Environmental (org-specific impact).$$),
('cy-f-038','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$In CVSS, what do AV, AC, PR, and UI stand for?$$,$$Attack Vector, Attack Complexity, Privileges Required, User Interaction - base exploitability metrics. Network AV + low complexity + no privileges/UI = highest risk.$$),
('cy-f-039','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$Why should CVSS not be the sole prioritization input?$$,$$It ignores your exposure, asset value, and active exploitation. A medium CVSS on an internet-facing crown jewel can outrank a critical on an isolated box.$$),
('cy-f-040','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is CISA KEV and why does it matter for prioritization?$$,$$The Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog lists flaws under active exploitation - patch these first regardless of CVSS, since attackers are using them now.$$),
('cy-f-041','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is EPSS?$$,$$Exploit Prediction Scoring System - estimates the probability a vulnerability will be exploited in the wild, complementing CVSS severity with likelihood.$$),
('cy-f-042','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between CVE, CWE, and CPE?$$,$$CVE: a specific vulnerability instance. CWE: the weakness type/class (e.g., CWE-89 SQLi). CPE: standardized platform/product naming.$$),
('cy-f-043','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is SCAP?$$,$$Security Content Automation Protocol - standards (OVAL, XCCDF, CVE, CPE) for automated, vendor-neutral configuration and vulnerability assessment.$$),
('cy-f-044','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is SAST vs DAST?$$,$$SAST analyzes source/binary without running it (early, whole codebase, false positives). DAST tests the running application (real behavior, later stage).$$),
('cy-f-045','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is IAST and how does it differ from SAST/DAST?$$,$$Interactive Application Security Testing instruments the running app to observe code execution during tests - combining SAST insight with DAST realism.$$),
('cy-f-046','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is fuzzing?$$,$$Feeding malformed/random inputs to an application to trigger crashes or unexpected behavior that reveal vulnerabilities.$$),
('cy-f-047','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is SCA (Software Composition Analysis)?$$,$$Scanning third-party/open-source dependencies for known vulnerabilities and license issues - addressing supply-chain risk in the software you build on.$$),
('cy-f-048','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What are common remediation-inhibiting factors?$$,$$Legacy systems, business-critical uptime requirements, MOUs/SLAs, degrading functionality, and organizational governance/approval delays.$$),
('cy-f-049','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$What is compensating control vs remediation vs mitigation?$$,$$Remediation fully fixes (patch). Mitigation reduces likelihood/impact. Compensating control substitutes an alternative when the fix isn't feasible.$$),
('cy-f-050','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is a false positive in scanning and why is validation needed?$$,$$A reported flaw that doesn't actually exist/apply. Validation (manual review, credentialed rescan) prevents wasted remediation and alert fatigue.$$),
('cy-f-051','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is asset criticality and how does it affect scanning?$$,$$How essential an asset is to the business - drives scan frequency, prioritization, and whether disruptive active scanning is acceptable.$$),
('cy-f-052','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What are OWASP Top 10 categories a CySA+ analyst should know?$$,$$Broken access control, cryptographic failures, injection, insecure design, security misconfiguration, vulnerable components, auth failures, integrity failures, logging failures, SSRF.$$),
('cy-f-053','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is SSRF and why is it dangerous in cloud?$$,$$Server-Side Request Forgery tricks a server into making requests on the attacker's behalf - in cloud it can reach the metadata service to steal credentials.$$),
('cy-f-054','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is insecure deserialization?$$,$$Untrusted serialized data processed by an app can lead to remote code execution or object injection - mitigate by validating/signing serialized data.$$),
('cy-f-055','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is a race condition (TOCTOU) vulnerability?$$,$$Time-of-check to time-of-use: a resource changes between validation and use, letting an attacker slip in a malicious substitution.$$),
('cy-f-056','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is privilege escalation, vertical vs horizontal?$$,$$Vertical: gaining higher privileges (user to admin). Horizontal: accessing another user's data at the same privilege level.$$),
('cy-f-057','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is the role of a bug bounty in vulnerability management?$$,$$Crowdsourced, continuous testing where external researchers are paid for validated findings under responsible-disclosure rules.$$),
('cy-f-058','cysa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is attack surface management?$$,$$Continuously discovering, inventorying, and monitoring all internet-facing assets/entry points to shrink exploitable exposure.$$),
('cy-f-059','cysa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is vulnerability chaining?$$,$$Combining multiple lower-severity flaws into a high-impact attack path - why individual CVSS scores can understate real risk.$$),
('cy-f-060','cysa','core',2,'2.4',$$Why maintain a maintenance window and change control for patching?$$,$$To apply fixes with testing, approval, and a rollback plan during low-impact periods - avoiding outages and unreviewed security regressions.$$),
('cy-f-061','cysa','core',2,'2.3',$$What is IaC scanning?$$,$$Scanning Infrastructure-as-Code templates (Terraform, CloudFormation) for misconfigurations before deployment - shifting security left.$$),
('cy-f-062','cysa','core',2,'2.5',$$What is directory traversal?$$,$$Using ../ sequences in input to access files outside the intended directory (e.g., /etc/passwd). Mitigate with input validation and least privilege.$$),
-- Domain 3: Incident Response & Management
('cy-f-063','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What are the phases of the NIST incident response lifecycle?$$,$$Preparation; Detection & Analysis; Containment, Eradication & Recovery; Post-Incident Activity. It is a loop - lessons feed back to preparation.$$),
('cy-f-064','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the difference between an event and an incident?$$,$$An event is any observable occurrence. An incident is an event (or series) that violates security policy or threatens CIA and requires response.$$),
('cy-f-065','cysa','core',3,'3.2',$$What distinguishes containment, eradication, and recovery?$$,$$Containment stops the spread (isolate). Eradication removes the cause (malware, accounts, vulnerability). Recovery restores validated systems to production.$$),
('cy-f-066','cysa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is short-term vs long-term containment?$$,$$Short-term: immediate isolation to stop damage. Long-term: hardened temporary fixes that let business continue while a permanent rebuild is prepared.$$),
('cy-f-067','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the order of volatility in evidence collection?$$,$$Most to least volatile: CPU registers/cache, RAM, network state, running processes, disk, then logs/backups. Collect the most volatile first.$$),
('cy-f-068','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is chain of custody?$$,$$A documented, unbroken record of who handled evidence, when, and how - preserving integrity and admissibility in legal proceedings.$$),
('cy-f-069','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$Why use a write blocker in forensics?$$,$$It prevents any modification of the source media during imaging, preserving evidence integrity (you analyze the copy, not the original).$$),
('cy-f-070','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is forensic imaging and hashing?$$,$$Creating a bit-for-bit copy of media and hashing (e.g., SHA-256) both source and copy to prove they are identical and unaltered.$$),
('cy-f-071','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a playbook vs a runbook in IR?$$,$$Playbook: decision-oriented response plan for a scenario (e.g., ransomware). Runbook: step-by-step operational procedure. Playbooks contain runbooks.$$),
('cy-f-072','cysa','core',3,'3.4',$$What is root cause analysis and why is it done post-incident?$$,$$Determining the underlying cause (not just symptoms) so remediation prevents the whole class of incident from recurring.$$),
('cy-f-073','cysa','core',3,'3.4',$$What is a lessons-learned / after-action review?$$,$$A blameless post-incident meeting documenting what happened, what worked, and concrete improvements - closing the loop into preparation.$$),
('cy-f-074','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What are the roles in a CSIRT?$$,$$Incident commander, analysts, forensics, legal, communications/PR, HR, and management liaison - defined before an incident so response isn't ad hoc.$$),
('cy-f-075','cysa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is scoping in incident analysis?$$,$$Determining the full extent of a compromise - which systems, accounts, and data are affected - before eradicating, so nothing is missed.$$),
('cy-f-076','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is memory (RAM) forensics used to recover?$$,$$Running processes, network connections, injected code, encryption keys, and fileless malware that never touched disk - lost at power-off.$$),
('cy-f-077','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a tabletop exercise?$$,$$A discussion-based walkthrough of an incident scenario to test plans and roles without touching production - cheap way to find gaps.$$),
('cy-f-078','cysa','core',3,'3.4',$$What legal/regulatory obligations can follow an incident?$$,$$Breach notification laws (GDPR 72h, state laws), regulatory reporting, evidence preservation/legal hold, and contractual notification duties.$$),
('cy-f-079','cysa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the difference between isolation, segmentation, and removal in containment?$$,$$Isolation: cut a host off. Segmentation: restrict network zones. Removal: take the affected system offline entirely. Chosen by severity and business need.$$),
('cy-f-080','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What are timelines and how are they built in IR?$$,$$Chronological reconstructions of attacker activity from correlated log/artifact timestamps - essential for scoping and root cause.$$),
('cy-f-081','cysa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is severity/impact classification in incident triage?$$,$$Ranking incidents by business impact and urgency (data loss, systems affected, regulatory exposure) to allocate response resources appropriately.$$),
('cy-f-082','cysa','core',3,'3.3',$$What is data carving in forensics?$$,$$Recovering files from raw disk/memory based on content signatures (headers/footers) even when file-system metadata is missing or deleted.$$),
-- Domain 4: Reporting & Communication
('cy-f-083','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$Why does vulnerability reporting need to be tailored to the audience?$$,$$Executives need risk/business impact and trends; technical teams need specific findings and remediation steps. One format doesn't serve both.$$),
('cy-f-084','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What are common vulnerability management KPIs/metrics?$$,$$Mean time to remediate (MTTR), scan coverage, number of criticals open, SLA compliance, recurrence rate, and trend over time.$$),
('cy-f-085','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is an SLA in the context of vulnerability remediation?$$,$$A defined timeframe to remediate by severity (e.g., criticals in 15 days) - reporting tracks compliance against it.$$),
('cy-f-086','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the purpose of a risk score in reporting?$$,$$To combine severity, exposure, and asset value into a single prioritized number stakeholders can act on and track over time.$$),
('cy-f-087','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is stakeholder identification in incident communication?$$,$$Determining who must be informed (management, legal, PR, affected users, regulators, law enforcement) and what each needs to know, when.$$),
('cy-f-088','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is a communication plan in incident response?$$,$$Predefined who-tells-whom-what-when, including escalation paths, out-of-band channels, and holding statements - so messaging isn't improvised mid-crisis.$$),
('cy-f-089','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$Why use out-of-band communication during an incident?$$,$$If the primary network/email is compromised, attackers could read response coordination - so teams switch to separate, trusted channels.$$),
('cy-f-090','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between an incident declaration and escalation?$$,$$Declaration: formally recognizing an event as an incident, triggering the plan. Escalation: raising severity/involvement as impact grows.$$),
('cy-f-091','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is a compliance report and who consumes it?$$,$$Evidence that controls meet a standard (PCI DSS, HIPAA, SOC 2) - consumed by auditors, regulators, and customers.$$),
('cy-f-092','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the value of trend analysis in security reporting?$$,$$Showing whether posture is improving or degrading over time (recurring vulns, MTTR direction) - informs investment and process changes.$$),
('cy-f-093','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is a root cause section in an incident report for?$$,$$To document the underlying failure and the corrective actions, so leadership can approve systemic fixes and prevent recurrence.$$),
('cy-f-094','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What are inhibitors to remediation that reporting should surface?$$,$$Business process interruption, degrading functionality, legacy systems, MOUs/SLAs, and organizational governance - stakeholders must weigh these.$$),
('cy-f-095','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is action item / recommendation tracking in reporting?$$,$$Assigning owners and due dates to findings and following them to closure - reporting without follow-through changes nothing.$$),
('cy-f-096','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$Why is a blameless culture important in incident reporting?$$,$$Fear of blame suppresses honest reporting and hides root causes; blameless reviews get the truth needed to actually improve.$$),
('cy-f-097','cysa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the difference between a metric and a KPI?$$,$$A metric is any measurement; a KPI is a metric tied to a goal that indicates performance (e.g., % criticals remediated within SLA).$$),
('cy-f-098','cysa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is evidence retention and why does it matter for reporting?$$,$$Keeping logs, images, and artifacts per policy/legal hold so reports are defensible and investigations can be revisited or used in court.$$),
-- acronyms
('cy-a-001','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$IOC$$,$$Indicator of Compromise - artifact (hash, IP, domain, behavior) showing a breach occurred.$$),
('cy-a-002','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$IOA$$,$$Indicator of Attack - behavior/intent signaling an attack in progress, independent of specific artifacts.$$),
('cy-a-003','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$TTP$$,$$Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures - durable behavioral patterns of a threat actor.$$),
('cy-a-004','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$ATT&CK$$,$$Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge - MITRE's matrix of real-world adversary behavior.$$),
('cy-a-005','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$STIX$$,$$Structured Threat Information eXpression - standardized language for cyber threat intelligence.$$),
('cy-a-006','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$TAXII$$,$$Trusted Automated eXchange of Intelligence Information - transport protocol for sharing STIX data.$$),
('cy-a-007','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$UEBA$$,$$User and Entity Behavior Analytics - anomaly detection from behavioral baselines.$$),
('cy-a-008','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$SOAR$$,$$Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response - automated playbook execution across tools.$$),
('cy-a-009','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$EDR$$,$$Endpoint Detection and Response - endpoint telemetry, detection, and response.$$),
('cy-a-010','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$XDR$$,$$Extended Detection and Response - detection correlated across endpoint, network, cloud, email.$$),
('cy-a-011','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$SIEM$$,$$Security Information and Event Management - log aggregation, correlation, and alerting.$$),
('cy-a-012','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$C2$$,$$Command and Control - attacker infrastructure that directs compromised hosts.$$),
('cy-a-013','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$LOTL$$,$$Living Off The Land - abusing legitimate built-in tools to evade detection.$$),
('cy-a-014','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$DLP$$,$$Data Loss Prevention - detects/blocks sensitive data leaving the organization.$$),
('cy-a-015','cysa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$OSINT$$,$$Open-Source Intelligence - intelligence gathered from publicly available sources.$$),
('cy-a-016','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$CVSS$$,$$Common Vulnerability Scoring System - 0-10 severity scoring for vulnerabilities.$$),
('cy-a-017','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$CVE$$,$$Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures - unique identifier for a specific known vulnerability.$$),
('cy-a-018','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$CWE$$,$$Common Weakness Enumeration - classification of software weakness types.$$),
('cy-a-019','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$CPE$$,$$Common Platform Enumeration - standardized naming for platforms and products.$$),
('cy-a-020','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$KEV$$,$$Known Exploited Vulnerabilities - CISA catalog of actively exploited flaws to prioritize.$$),
('cy-a-021','cysa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$EPSS$$,$$Exploit Prediction Scoring System - probability a vulnerability will be exploited.$$),
('cy-a-022','cysa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$SCAP$$,$$Security Content Automation Protocol - standards for automated security assessment.$$),
('cy-a-023','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$SAST$$,$$Static Application Security Testing - analyzing code without executing it.$$),
('cy-a-024','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$DAST$$,$$Dynamic Application Security Testing - testing a running application.$$),
('cy-a-025','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$IAST$$,$$Interactive Application Security Testing - instrumented testing of a running app.$$),
('cy-a-026','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$SCA$$,$$Software Composition Analysis - scanning third-party/open-source components.$$),
('cy-a-027','cysa','acronym',2,'2.5',$$SSRF$$,$$Server-Side Request Forgery - coercing a server to make attacker-controlled requests.$$),
('cy-a-028','cysa','acronym',2,'2.3',$$IaC$$,$$Infrastructure as Code - defining infrastructure in versioned, scannable templates.$$),
('cy-a-029','cysa','acronym',3,'3.3',$$CoC$$,$$Chain of Custody - documented handling record preserving evidence integrity.$$),
('cy-a-030','cysa','acronym',3,'3.1',$$CSIRT$$,$$Computer Security Incident Response Team - the group that handles incidents.$$),
('cy-a-031','cysa','acronym',3,'3.4',$$RCA$$,$$Root Cause Analysis - identifying the underlying cause to prevent recurrence.$$),
('cy-a-032','cysa','acronym',3,'3.1',$$IR$$,$$Incident Response - the structured process of handling security incidents.$$),
('cy-a-033','cysa','acronym',3,'3.4',$$MTTD$$,$$Mean Time To Detect - average time from compromise to detection.$$),
('cy-a-034','cysa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$MTTR$$,$$Mean Time To Respond/Remediate - average time to contain or fix an issue.$$),
('cy-a-035','cysa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$SLA$$,$$Service Level Agreement - committed remediation/response timeframes.$$),
('cy-a-036','cysa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$KPI$$,$$Key Performance Indicator - a goal-linked metric of performance.$$),
('cy-a-037','cysa','acronym',4,'4.2',$$RTO$$,$$Recovery Time Objective - maximum tolerable time to restore a service.$$),
('cy-a-038','cysa','acronym',4,'4.2',$$RPO$$,$$Recovery Point Objective - maximum tolerable data loss measured in time.$$),
('cy-a-039','cysa','acronym',2,'2.5',$$RCE$$,$$Remote Code Execution - running arbitrary attacker code on a target.$$),
('cy-a-040','cysa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$PCAP$$,$$Packet Capture - full recording of network traffic for analysis.$$),
-- feynman
('cy-y-001','cysa','feynman',2,'2.2',$$Explain why two vulnerabilities with the same CVSS score can have very different real-world priority.$$,$$Model answer: CVSS measures intrinsic severity, not your situation. Priority also depends on exposure (internet-facing vs isolated), whether it's being actively exploited (CISA KEV / high EPSS), the value of the affected asset, and whether it chains with other flaws. A CVSS 7 on an exposed, exploited payment server outranks a CVSS 9 on an air-gapped test box.$$),
('cy-y-002','cysa','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain order of volatility and why a responder captures RAM before imaging the disk.$$,$$Model answer: Evidence disappears at different rates. RAM holds running processes, network connections, encryption keys, and fileless malware - all gone the instant you pull power. Disk persists. So you collect most-volatile first (memory) before shutting down or imaging the disk, or you lose the most fragile and often most revealing evidence.$$),
('cy-y-003','cysa','feynman',1,'1.2',$$Explain the pyramid of pain to a junior analyst deciding what to block.$$,$$Model answer: Indicators differ in how much they hurt an attacker to lose. Blocking a file hash is trivial for them to change (recompile). IPs and domains cost a bit more. Tools cost real effort. But detecting their TTPs - how they actually operate - forces them to relearn their playbook. Detections built on TTPs are durable; those built on hashes expire almost immediately.$$),
('cy-y-004','cysa','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain the difference between containment, eradication, and recovery using a house-fire analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Containment is closing doors to stop the fire spreading room to room. Eradication is putting the fire out and removing what's still smoldering (the malware, attacker accounts, the vulnerability they used). Recovery is rebuilding and repainting the damaged rooms and moving back in - only after you've confirmed the fire is truly out, or it reignites.$$),
('cy-y-005','cysa','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain why a credentialed scan finds more than a non-credentialed scan, and the trade-off.$$,$$Model answer: A non-credentialed scan sees only what's exposed from outside - the attacker's view, but shallow and full of guesses (false positives). A credentialed scan logs in and reads installed versions, patch levels, and configs directly, so it's far more accurate and complete. The trade-off is you must manage and protect those scan credentials, since they're powerful.$$),
('cy-y-006','cysa','feynman',4,'4.2',$$Explain why incident responders sometimes communicate out-of-band.$$,$$Model answer: If attackers are in your email or network, any response you coordinate there can be read by them - they'll see you closing in and accelerate or hide. Out-of-band means a separate, trusted channel (phone tree, separate messaging) the attacker can't access, so your team can coordinate containment without tipping them off.$$),
('cy-y-007','cysa','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain how beaconing reveals a compromised host even when the malware is hidden.$$,$$Model answer: Malware usually phones home to a command server on a schedule. Even if the payload hides on the host, that network pattern is visible: regular, similar-sized connections to the same external destination at fixed intervals. Flow analysis spots the rhythm - real user traffic is bursty and varied, beacons are metronomic.$$),
('cy-y-008','cysa','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain SSRF and why it's especially dangerous in cloud environments.$$,$$Model answer: SSRF tricks a server into making requests the attacker chooses. Normally bad, but in cloud it's worse: the server can reach the instance metadata endpoint (a special internal address) that hands out temporary IAM credentials. So an SSRF bug becomes credential theft, letting the attacker act as the server's cloud role.$$),
('cy-y-009','cysa','feynman',3,'3.4',$$Explain why a blameless post-incident review produces better security than finding who to fire.$$,$$Model answer: If people expect punishment, they hide mistakes, omit details, and defend themselves - so you never learn the real root cause and it happens again. Blameless review treats the incident as a systems failure: what conditions allowed it? People tell the truth, you find the actual gap (missing control, unclear process), and you fix the system, not the scapegoat.$$),
('cy-y-010','cysa','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain how you'd report the same vulnerability finding differently to an executive vs an engineer.$$,$$Model answer: The engineer needs specifics: the CVE, affected hosts, exact remediation steps, and a deadline. The executive needs the business translation: how much risk this represents, whether it's trending better or worse, what it would cost to fix vs the cost of a breach, and a yes/no decision to make. Same finding, two languages - technical detail for action, risk and money for decisions.$$);
